WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.160
Day 18 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.497
How to test a model: accuracy, N runs and ranges.

00:00:09.047 --> 00:00:13.621
How to measure a decision-making model (an agent mapping messy names to a master

00:00:13.621 --> 00:00:17.223
catalog) with repeated runs, ranges and per-error-type metrics.

00:00:18.523 --> 00:00:23.117
"Which model is best for this?" is a question almost always answered with a public

00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:26.702
leaderboard or the impression of whoever tried it one afternoon.

00:00:27.002 --> 00:00:31.602
Neither says how it will behave on your task, with your data and your prompt.

00:00:31.902 --> 00:00:36.921
And when someone does measure it, they usually do it with a single run and report a

00:00:36.921 --> 00:00:37.768
single number.

00:00:39.068 --> 00:00:43.192
Testing a model seriously is closer to an experiment than to a test.

00:00:43.492 --> 00:00:48.512
There is a defined task, a fixed set, several repetitions and a result with its

00:00:48.512 --> 00:00:48.893
range.

00:00:49.193 --> 00:00:53.385
This guide uses a record-mapping case to show the full method: what to measure

00:00:53.385 --> 00:00:57.739
besides accuracy, how to tell a real difference from noise and how to decide when

00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:00.318
the most expensive model isn't clearly the best.

00:01:00.618 --> 00:01:02.804
The figures shown are illustrative.

00:01:04.104 --> 00:01:05.801
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:06.351 --> 00:01:10.243
The golden dataset from day 17 (about 100 test cases).

00:01:10.793 --> 00:01:14.674
An eval harness with a pinned model version (days 8 and 15).

00:01:15.974 --> 00:01:17.253
This is the setup.

00:01:17.553 --> 00:01:21.770
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.337
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:26.637 --> 00:01:27.673
Step 1.

00:01:27.973 --> 00:01:29.891
Task and actions defined.

00:01:30.441 --> 00:01:33.625
Define the task and possible actions precisely.

00:01:33.925 --> 00:01:38.804
In this case: map to an existing record, approve as new or send to review.

00:01:39.354 --> 00:01:43.733
Write down precisely what the model receives and which are the only valid outputs.

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:48.977
In the sample case a new record arrives and there are three possible actions: map it

00:01:48.977 --> 00:01:52.802
to an existing one, approve it as new or send it to human review.

00:01:53.102 --> 00:01:56.808
Defining the actions as a closed list makes the task measurable.

00:01:57.108 --> 00:02:01.813
It also forces a decision on what is expected in each doubtful situation.

00:02:02.113 --> 00:02:06.885
If the definition is vague, you'll be measuring the model's interpretation and not

00:02:06.885 --> 00:02:07.758
its capability.

00:02:09.058 --> 00:02:10.256
Step 2.

00:02:10.556 --> 00:02:12.614
Five runs per configuration.

00:02:13.164 --> 00:02:16.591
Run each configuration 5 times on the same test set.

00:02:16.891 --> 00:02:18.926
A single run isn't a measurement.

00:02:19.476 --> 00:02:24.286
Run each configuration five times on the same test set, changing nothing between

00:02:24.286 --> 00:02:24.587
runs.

00:02:24.887 --> 00:02:29.243
The same model on the same input can move several points from one run to the next.

00:02:29.543 --> 00:02:34.250
If you measure once, you don't know whether you saw its best day, its worst or a

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:34.956
typical one.

00:02:35.256 --> 00:02:40.112
Five repetitions is a reasonable trade-off between cost and information.

00:02:40.412 --> 00:02:44.817
If results come out widely spread, that spread is already a finding about the

00:02:44.817 --> 00:02:46.591
model's stability on your task.

00:02:47.891 --> 00:02:48.973
Step 3.

00:02:49.273 --> 00:02:50.611
Mean and range.

00:02:51.161 --> 00:02:53.857
Report mean and range (min and max).

00:02:54.157 --> 00:02:58.084
If two configurations overlap in range, the difference is noise.

00:02:58.634 --> 00:03:02.990
Report the mean together with the minimum and maximum of the five runs.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:08.345
Then apply a simple rule: if the ranges of two configurations overlap, you can't

00:03:08.345 --> 00:03:09.609
claim one is better.

00:03:09.909 --> 00:03:12.431
That difference is compatible with noise.

00:03:12.731 --> 00:03:16.998
Many decisions to switch model or prompt are made on one- or two-point differences

00:03:16.998 --> 00:03:19.339
that vanish when the measurement is repeated.

00:03:19.639 --> 00:03:23.705
Showing the range in the table avoids that argument before it starts.

00:03:25.005 --> 00:03:26.018
Step 4.

00:03:26.318 --> 00:03:27.842
Beyond accuracy.

00:03:28.392 --> 00:03:33.480
Look beyond accuracy: per-action precision and number of costly errors per run.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:39.177
A wrong map to an existing record pollutes data; over-sending to review only costs

00:03:39.177 --> 00:03:39.506
time.

00:03:40.056 --> 00:03:43.193
Accuracy treats all errors as equal, and they aren't.

00:03:43.493 --> 00:03:48.379
In the example, mapping a record to an existing one that doesn't correspond pollutes

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:50.531
the data and is hard to detect later.

00:03:50.831 --> 00:03:54.166
Over-sending to review only costs a person's time.

00:03:54.466 --> 00:03:58.951
That's why it's worth looking at per-action precision and, above all, counting

00:03:58.951 --> 00:04:00.216
costly errors per run.

00:04:00.516 --> 00:04:05.435
A model with slightly lower accuracy and half the costly errors may be the best

00:04:05.435 --> 00:04:05.871
choice.

00:04:07.171 --> 00:04:08.299
Step 5.

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:11.365
Same conditions, quality against cost.

00:04:11.915 --> 00:04:16.539
Compare models with the same prompt and dataset, quality against cost per call.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:22.304
To compare models, hold everything else fixed: the same prompt, the same dataset,

00:04:22.304 --> 00:04:25.845
the same parameters and the same judge if there is one.

00:04:26.145 --> 00:04:28.992
Add cost per call and latency to the table.

00:04:29.292 --> 00:04:33.699
The useful comparison isn't which model has the highest number but how much

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:36.284
additional quality you get per unit of cost.

00:04:36.584 --> 00:04:41.367
Sometimes that additional quality exists, and sometimes it sits within the noise

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:43.460
margin, at several times the price.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:45.947
Step 6.

00:04:46.247 --> 00:04:48.002
Decide with the full table.

00:04:48.552 --> 00:04:50.308
Decide with the full table.

00:04:50.608 --> 00:04:55.492
The most expensive model doesn't always win, and sometimes wins by less than the

00:04:55.492 --> 00:04:55.859
noise.

00:04:56.409 --> 00:05:01.555
The decision comes from reading all the columns together: mean, range, costly

00:05:01.555 --> 00:05:03.226
errors, cost and latency.

00:05:03.526 --> 00:05:08.217
The most expensive doesn't always win, and when it does it may win by less than the

00:05:08.217 --> 00:05:08.556
noise.

00:05:08.856 --> 00:05:12.434
Leave the decision in writing together with the table and the run card.

00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:17.283
Three months from now, when a new model comes out, you'll be able to repeat exactly

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:21.886
the same experiment and compare against a reference of your own, instead of starting

00:05:21.886 --> 00:05:23.256
the discussion from zero.

00:05:24.556 --> 00:05:25.661
An example.

00:05:26.211 --> 00:05:31.258
With made-up figures to illustrate the method: a team compares three configurations

00:05:31.258 --> 00:05:33.447
on forty test cases, five runs each.

00:05:33.747 --> 00:05:40.205
The small model averages 71% with a range of 68 to 75 and six costly errors per run.

00:05:40.505 --> 00:05:46.463
The medium one, 84% with a range of 81 to 86 and two costly errors.

00:05:46.763 --> 00:05:52.766
The large one, 85% with a range of 82 to 88, also two costly errors and five times

00:05:52.766 --> 00:05:54.742
the cost of the medium one.

00:05:55.042 --> 00:05:59.933
The medium and large ranges overlap almost entirely, so the one-point difference

00:05:59.933 --> 00:06:00.850
isn't evidence.

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:05.531
They choose the medium one and document that the large one didn't justify its cost

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:06.226
on this task.

00:06:07.526 --> 00:06:09.189
The most common mistakes.

00:06:09.739 --> 00:06:11.970
Deciding with a single run per model.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:14.636
Reporting a mean without its range.

00:06:15.186 --> 00:06:18.718
Looking only at accuracy when errors have different costs.

00:06:19.268 --> 00:06:22.382
Comparing models with different prompts or datasets.

00:06:23.682 --> 00:06:25.926
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:26.476 --> 00:06:29.532
The task has a closed list of valid actions.

00:06:30.082 --> 00:06:33.637
Each configuration ran five times on the same test set.

00:06:34.187 --> 00:06:38.915
The table shows mean, range, costly errors, cost and latency.

00:06:39.465 --> 00:06:42.939
The decision is written down next to the table and the run card.

00:06:44.239 --> 00:06:46.134
And where AI fell short.

00:06:46.684 --> 00:06:51.781
The same model on the same input varies several points between runs, and some models

00:06:51.781 --> 00:06:53.722
fail outright on large contexts.

00:06:54.022 --> 00:06:56.498
A single number doesn't describe a model.

00:06:57.798 --> 00:06:59.356
That was day 18.

00:06:59.656 --> 00:07:04.631
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:04.631 --> 00:07:05.638
asked questions.

00:07:06.188 --> 00:07:11.822
Continue with day 19: Cost as a quality metric: top-K prefiltering before the model.
